Seebeck coefficient is a dynamic parameter to decide the power factor and figure of merit as it is openly associ- ated to these factors. More the Seebeck coefficient more efficient will be the TE material. The exploration of the graphical deviation of Seebeck coefficient is portrayed in Figure 7, which reflects that the hole as majority trans- porters in both spins as S is positive in the entire range of temperature in Co2ScSb. In up-spin channel S increases linearly from 3.04 μV/K at 50 K to 12.78 μV/K at 800 K, while there is exponential decay in S in spin down chan- nel with temperature that is, 261.54 μV/K at 50 K to 236.68 μV/K at 800 K. Here in our case Seebeck coeffi- cient is positive designates that holes are as majority car- riers and which descripts p-type band structure of Co2ScSb alloy.

From Figure 7 Seebeck coefficient is negative in up- spin, demonstrating the occurrence of n-type charge packets. The modulus of S increases from −3.10 μVK−1 at 50 K to −20.10 μVK−1 at 800 K for up-spin. In down-spin, S decreases from −574.67 μVK−1 at 50 K to −216.88 μVK−1 at 800 K. The Seebeck coefficient is negative describes that electrons are as majority carriers and which exhibits n-type band structure of Co2TiSb alloy. The total Seebeck coefficient is computed by using two current model33 illustrated as

\begin{equation}
S=\frac{\sigma^{\uparrow}S^{\uparrow}+\sigma^{\downarrow}S^{\downarrow}}{\sigma^{\uparrow}+\sigma^{\downarrow}}
\end{equation}

