Another important aspect is the calculation of power factor (PF) which is the noteworthy parameter to evalu- ate the efficiency of the material. The thermo-power and PF are directly dependent on each other as, PF = S2σ where the high value of thermo-power results in high output generated from the material. The power factor

indicates the associated electrons that perform an essen- tial role in improving the TE response. Figure 7 displays the variation of PF with temperature and hints an increases trend. The calculated value of PF is 1.56 and 1.80 W/mK2s at room temperature for Co2ScSb and Co2TiSb, respectively. Therefore, a material with high electrical conductivity and high thermo-power will be the first priority for the selection in thermoelectric technology.

The graphical variation of the total thermal conductivity is displayed in Figure 8. The electronic part of the thermal conductivity delivers that it increases with the increase in temperature and increases from 2.98 WK−1 m−1 at 50 K to 6.81WK−1 m−1 800 K for Co2ScSb and 3.63 WK−1 m−1 at 300 K to 20.68 WK−1 m−1 at 800 K for Co2TiSb. It hints a linearly rising trend because of the semiconducting perfor- mance in up and down channels. Lattice conductivity decreases from 13.42 WK−1 m−1 at 50 K to 7.38 WK−1 m−1 at 800 K for Co2ScSb and 9.32 to 0.45 WK−1 m−1 at 800 K for Co2TiSb. At room temperature the total thermal conduc- tivity (κ) for Co2ScSb is 5.46 WK−1 m−1 and that of Co2TiSb is 9.09 WK−1 m−1.

\begin{title}
3.6 | Thermal properties
\end{title}

We have tried to describe the nature of stability of these materials using various thermodynamic potentials. The use of quasi-harmonic Debye model34,35 which takes into consideration of calculating the approximate value of these thermodynamic potentials. The specific heat at con- stant volume (Cv) is one of the prime factor of the mate- rial relating the carrier capability of the waste heat from source. It also relates the cause and effect of dynamics and microstructures of the material. Here the graphical variation in Figure 9 shows that the material possesses greater capability to transport heat with T3 relation up to room temperature. The method of calculation under anharmonic scheme hints to the robust dependency of the specific heat on the temperature. The high tempera- ture limit suggests that the change attains a constant value and follows Dulong-Petit law.36

The nature of bonding among the constituent atoms can be understood through the behavior of thermal expansion coefficient (α) against the temperature is illus- trated in Figure 10. The consequence of pressure approves that there is decrease in (α) with increasing pressure. Similarly, we see an exponential increasing trend with temperature at a particular pressure.

The Grüneisen parameter (γ) demonstrates the under- standing between the frequency of phonon and change of volume. One can observed from Figure 11 at constant temperatures, a drastic decrease in γ on raising the

pressure. At constant pressure, the Grüneisen parameter rises softly with the increasing temperature. The obtained values of γ at 300 K and 0 GPa are 2.21 for Co2ScSb and 2.60 for Co2TiSb. Meanwhile, Co2XSb Heuslers delivers decent γ values, indicates a strong anharmonicity preva- iling in these compounds.

\begin{title}
4 | CONCLUSION
\end{title}

High-throughput calculations based on DFT are investi- gating the magneto-electronic, structural, mechanical, and thermophysical properties of Co2ScSb and Co2TiSb Heuslers. Structural Optimization and elastic parameters confirms the geometrically and dynamically stability in cubic Fm 3m phase. The calculation through the approach of exchange correlation functional modified Becke Johnson (mBJ) exhibits the half-metallicity of Co2XSb in-spite of GGA and GGA + U approximation. The conforming bands expose the n-type for Co2ScSb possessing energy gap of 0.84 eV and p-type conduction for Co2TiSb delivers the gap of 1.04 eV, exhibiting both indirect band-gaps. Total magnetic moment equal to 2μB which is an integer value and recommend the character- istic property of half metallic materials. Moreover, the mechanical parameters clarify the ductile character of both these alloys. The transport properties were checked to scrutinize the semiconducting character. These mate- rials display decent Seebeck coefficient and power factor at room temperature for this reason they could be appli- cable for various fundamental applications in spintronics, spin injectors, thermoelectrics. Furthermore, the thermo- dynamic coefficients descript the better stability toward high pressure and temperature.

