\begin{title}
3.2 | Structural stability
\end{title}

Structural properties reveal a crucial role in predicting the atomic occupancy at different lattice sites and also picture the bonding in between them. In this study, the materials display their structural arrangement of atoms in face-centred cubic Fm-3m symmetry. The Ba2MgMO6 (M = U, Np) consists of two sublattices crystalizes in a geometry in which four distinct elements in the ratio of 2:1:1:6 forming the DP unit cell structure mentioned in Figure 1. The unit cell of DP is twice the size of simple cubic perovskite cell. The position of larger cation Ba with 12-fold coordination resides at body centre having Wycoff 8c: (0.25,0.25,0.25), Mg and U/Np, which are responsible for the shape formation, are located at 4b: (0,0,0), 4a: (0.5,0.5,0.5) within the centre of O anion placed at 4e: (х,0,0), x = 0.25 at crystallographic corner sites forming alternate MgO6 and U/NpO6 octahedrons, respectively. The angle between Mg-O-U or Mg-O-Np is 180 . However, it is interesting to trace down the ground state energy for that Brich Murnaghan's equation,32 which is minimized in different magnetic spin-polarized (FM) and without spin-polarized (NM) phases to find its total energy/unit cell as a function of volume, which in terms of equation can be written as follows:

