\end{caption}

where n(e) represents the electronic DOS, f is the Fermi- Dirac distribution and eF is the Fermi level.

We have calculated the three terms of the right side of Eq. (1) as a function of volume. The equilibrium volume at any corresponding temperature T can be obtained by ﬁtting the Helmholtz free energy F versus T, V results to the Vient equation of states (EOS) [28]. Figure 3 illustrates the calculated F as a function of V between 0 and 800 K with a step of 100 K for L12-type, DO3-type, and DO19-type Fe3Ga, respectively. The equilibrium volumes are linked by dotted lines, as shown in the ﬁgures. Obviously, the equilibrium volume increases with the increasing tempera- ture. And on this basis, the relevant thermal properties such as V–T relationship, isothermal bulk modulus BT, and thermal expansion can be derived.

